// ⚠ `effects diverge` — the compiler cannot prove this loop terminates, and says so here
// rather than exempting every `proc` from the question. Before 2026-09-24 the keyword
// `proc` carried this permission silently: it means "may do IO", and every IO function
// inherited "may hang" for sharing it. Measured then: 173 procs did IO and terminated
// while exempt, against 61 that actually diverge. The marker is now on the exceptions.
// Both helpers return an unbounded i32 (the accumulator wraps), so their difference can
// overflow. The subject is the `and`-conjunct guard inside the loop, not the subtraction.
// A `while` guard's conjuncts each reach the body. `while i < 100 and s < 5` proves `a[i]`
// exactly as the bare `while i < 100` does.
//
// This is a LOWERING property, not a domain one, which is why it has its own test. Lowering a
// condition to a VALUE builds a bool temp written on two paths and a join before the branch,
// so the fact from the first conjunct is hulled against the path where it is false; ANY `and`
// in a while guard then lost BOTH conjuncts. The identical `and` in an `if` proved all along,
// because `if` already lowered its condition as CONTROL FLOW. Both now go through
// `ir_lower_cond_br`, which puts each conjunct's fact on its own edge.
//
// Both orders are checked: the index guard is the FIRST conjunct here and the SECOND below.
func first(a i32[100]) i32 effects diverge {
    var i usize = 0
    var s i32 = 0
    while i < 100 and s < 5 { s = s +% a[i]
        i = i + 1 }
    return s
}
func second(a i32[100]) i32 effects diverge {
    var i usize = 0
    var s i32 = 0
    while s < 5 and i < 100 { s = s +% a[i]
        i = i + 1 }
    return s
}
func main() i32 effects diverge {
    var xs i32[100] = [1 for k in 0..100]
    return first(xs) -% second(xs)
}
